{
  "name": "ZeroDrift Terminal Mk3 — Tier A",
  "note": "Single source of truth for the build BOM. docs/TERMINAL_MK3.md section 2 and docs/submission/ZeroDrift_Mk3_Build_Guide.pdf are both generated from this; tests/test_bom_consistency.py enforces it. Beacon runs from a USB power bank, not a 9V battery: the Nano's linear regulator wastes 9V->5V as heat and a 9V alkaline holds ~500mAh. Decoy runs from 2xAA, not a coin cell: a CR2032 is rated 0.2mA continuous and a high-brightness LED draws ~20mA, which is 100x over spec. Decoy resistor recomputed for a white LED (Vf ~3.1V): 150R against 2xAA's ~3V supply left under 1mA and went dark as the battery aged even slightly -- the opposite of 'brighter than the beacon'. 3xAA (4.5V nominal) + 68R gives 16-25mA across the pack's working life. Stage power: the demo runs from a laptop with no wall socket. The laptop's USB powers both Nanos, the camera, the laser, the encoders and the vibration motor. The only thing it cannot power is the pair of NEMA17 steppers -- the A4988 needs 8-35 V on VMOT and USB delivers 5 V. A 12 V Li-ion pack with a DC barrel output supplies that rail. A USB-PD trigger module was considered and rejected: it depends on the user's own power bank supporting PD, and 12 V is not even a guaranteed PD voltage (the standard's fixed steps are 5/9/15/20 V), so it makes the demo depend on hardware nobody has verified. The battery pack carries the same barrel connector the mains adapter used, so no other wiring changes. At ~1 A draw a 3000 mAh pack runs about three hours.",
